The studies GLV-12-301, HRP-301 (VERDANT-1) and HRP-205 (VERDANT-AC), the products GLV-12 and lumiravostat (Ostrevia),
the sponsors, sites, participants, subject identifiers and all results are fictional. The
adverse event and other coded terms come from a fictional study dictionary written for these
training apps; they are not MedDRA or WHODrug terms. Guidelines and templates (ICH E3 and its
Q&A (R1), ICH E9(R1), ICH M11, the CORE Reference, TransCelerate's CSR template, Regulation (EU)
No 536/2014, the New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules 2019) are named for education; the app
summarises them in its own words (the 16 Table 6 headings are named as the Rules name them) and does not reproduce their text. Nothing in it is regulatory
or legal advice.

The study outputs in src/data were produced by the reference programs of the TLF Programming
Lab, the patient-level SDTM by the SDTM Mapping Trainer, the HRP-301 safety cases and
reconciliation by the CDM Query & Edit-Check Console, and the protocols by the Protocol Builder
(all by the same author and under the same licence). HRP-205's outputs and protocol are made by
scripts/make-hrp-205.mjs; the reference safety information and Indian ethics committees are fictional, at
https://github.com/randomthingsonlineatsk-cloud/ (tlf-programming-lab, sdtm-mapping-trainer,
cdm-query-console, protocol-builder). Every participant, date of birth and event is fictional.

The app is not a validated system and must not hold real patient data. Everything stays in the
browser; there is no AI service: all checks and scores are by rules.
